Q: Powershell Issue with change Password A: <p>Can't test it at the moment, but I strongly suspect that <code>$account.psbase.invoke(&quot;setpassword&quot;, $newPassword)</code> returns <code>$null</code>, since you're not assigning or voiding it. <code>$account</code> is a <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/api/system.directoryservices.directoryentry" rel="nofollow noreferrer">DirectoryEntry</a> object, which can behave strangely. When that <code>$null</code> hits <code>Export-Csv</code>, the cmdlet doesn't know what to do with it.<br />
That aside, your output objects have inconsistent properties, and you're referencing an undefined variable <code>$Status</code>.<br />
There's also no need to store the results; use the <code>ForEach-Object</code> cmdlet and pipe the results directly to <code>Export-Csv</code>.<br />
Try something like this:</p>
<pre><code>$inFile = &quot;g:\computers.txt&quot;
$outFile = &quot;g:\password.csv&quot;

Function Get-RandomPassword {
    Param (
        [Parameter(Mandatory)]
        [int]$Length,
        [int]$AmountOfNonAlphanumeric = 1
    )
    Add-Type -AssemblyName 'System.Web'
    [System.Web.Security.Membership]::GeneratePassword($Length, $AmountOfNonAlphanumeric)
}

Get-Content -Path $inFile -ErrorAction Stop | ForEach-Object {
    $computer = $_
    Write-Host &quot;Processing $($computer)&quot;
    $result = [PSCustomObject]@{
        ComputerName   = $computer
        Status         = 'OFFLINE'
        Error          = $null
        NewCredentials = $null
    }
    If ((Test-Connection -ComputerName $computer -Count 1 -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue -ErrorVariable testError)) {
        Try {
            $newPassword = Get-RandomPassword -Length 12
            $account = [ADSI](&quot;WinNT://$($computer)/Administrator&quot;)
            [void]$account.psbase.Invoke('setpassword', $newPassword)

            $result.Status = 'OK'
            $result.NewCredentials = $newPassword
        } Catch {
            $result.Status = 'ERROR'
            $result.Error = $_.Exception.Message
        }
    } Else {
        # If Count in Test-Connection will always remain at 1, a simple &quot;$result.Error = $testError[0].Exception.Message&quot; will do.
        # This version will also handle multiple pings:
        $result.Error = ($testError | ForEach-Object {$_.Exception.Message} | Select-Object -Unique) -join '; '
    }
    $result
} | Export-Csv -Path $outFile -NoTypeInformation

</code></pre>
<p><strong>Edit:</strong> COM object will now be released.<br />
<strong>Edit2:</strong> $account is not a COM object after all (but DirectoryEntries are not a lot better) ...</p>

Q: Android google chrome give strange values on innerWidht/Height upon rotation and canvas resizing A: <p>Not seeing the logic here, but adding <code>minimum-scale=1</code> fixes my particular problem, hope this helps someone:</p>
<pre><code>  &lt;meta name=&quot;viewport&quot; content=&quot;width=device-width, initial-scale=1, minimum-scale=1&quot;&gt;
</code></pre>

Q: R shiny bslib sidebar and shinyWidgets pickerInput incompatible A: <p>Use <a href="https://rstudio.github.io/bslib/reference/page_sidebar.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>page_sidebar</code></a> instead of <code>fluidPage</code> - they are mutually exclusive; see the <a href="https://rstudio.github.io/bslib/reference/page_sidebar.html#ref-examples" rel="nofollow noreferrer">examples</a>.</p>
<pre><code>library(shiny)
library(bslib)


met_data &lt;- data.frame(
  STATE = rep(c(&quot;Alabama&quot;, &quot;Arkansas&quot;, &quot;Maine&quot;, &quot;Minnesota&quot;, &quot;Texas&quot;), 4),
  MET_VAR = rep(c('degC', 'rain', 'ice', 'humidity'), 5),
  RESULT = rnorm(20)
)

ui &lt;- page_sidebar( # replace page_fluid

  sidebar = sidebar( 
    style = &quot;position:fixed&quot;,
    bg = &quot;#C8CBD2&quot;,
    width = 350,
    h3(&quot;Example sidebar&quot;)
  ),

  mainPanel(
    width = 1400,

    # Input: choose state(s)
    shinyWidgets::pickerInput(
      inputId = 'stateInput',
      label = &quot;Select State&quot;,
      choices = sort(
        unique(
          met_data$STATE
        )
      ),
      options = list(`actions-box` = TRUE),
      multiple = TRUE
    ),

    # Input: choose met variable(s)
    shinyWidgets::pickerInput(
      inputId = 'Met_variable',
      label = &quot;Select Variable&quot;,
      choices = sort(
        unique(
          met_data$MET_VAR
        )
      ),
      options = list(`actions-box` = TRUE),
      multiple = TRUE
    ),

    DT::dataTableOutput(&quot;report_table&quot;)

  )
) 


server &lt;- function(input, output, session) {
  report_data &lt;- reactive({
    req(input$stateInput, input$Met_variable) # require inputs before using them
    met_data |&gt;
      subset(STATE %in% input$stateInput &amp;
               MET_VAR %in% input$Met_variable) |&gt;
      sort_by( ~ STATE) |&gt;
      transform(STATE = factor(STATE), MET_VAR = factor(MET_VAR))

  })  
  output$report_table &lt;- DT::renderDataTable({
    DT::datatable(
      report_data(),
      escape = FALSE,
      filter = &quot;top&quot;,
      options = list(scrollX = TRUE, autowidth = TRUE)
    )

  })

}

shinyApp(ui = ui, server = server)
</code></pre>

Q: Stop HTML table header from repeating on each printed page A: <p>The opposite question from 2008 linked by Sampson in his answer here contains curious hint that this functionality used to be controlled with setting <code>display</code> of the <code>thead</code> element, where some implementations needed explicit <code>table-header-group</code> value assignment to do what is expected: repeat these elements.  Turns out, in current browsers, we can actually use the similar mechanism to do the opposite: changing <code>thead</code>'s implicit display with</p>
<pre><code>thead { display: table-row-group; }
</code></pre>
<p>prevents it from repeating on consecutive continuation pages, without visually affecting its (single) appearance, both on computer screen and in the print (preview).  Presumably, it assigns it the same treatment like <code>tbody</code> has normally.</p>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="false" data-console="true" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="false" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code">
<pre class="snippet-code-css lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>thead {
 display: table-row-group;
}</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-html lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;table border&gt;
 &lt;caption&gt;Caption&lt;/caption&gt;
 &lt;thead&gt;
  &lt;tr&gt;
   &lt;th&gt;Header A&lt;/th&gt;
   &lt;th&gt;Header B&lt;/th&gt;
  &lt;/tr&gt;
 &lt;/thead&gt;
 &lt;tbody&gt;
  &lt;script&gt;
   for( var i = 1; i &lt; 500; i++ ) {
    document.write(
     `&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td&gt;row ${i} cell A
          &lt;td&gt;row ${i} cell B`
    )
   }
  &lt;/script&gt;
 &lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>

<p>Your browser should have right-click context menu with &quot;This frame&quot; submenu containing the &quot;Print&quot; option. If it does not, try <a href="http://myfonj.github.io/tst/long-table-no-header-repeat.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer">external <code>long-table-no-header-repeat.html</code> test page</a>, and possibly compare with regular untreated <a href="http://myfonj.github.io/tst/long-table.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>long-table.html</code></a>.</p>
<p>I am not sure whether changing the <code>thead</code>'s display changes its semantics for screen readers or whether it causes some other invisible side effects, so I would probably play it safe and change it only for <code>@media print {…}</code>.</p>
<p>Also interesting, that table's <code>caption</code> is not repeated. It can be made repeated by forcing it <code>display: table-header-group</code>, but that also stops the real header from cloning. (Tested in Firefox 149 and Edge 145.)</p>

Q: Value Binding Leaves Property Null A: <p>As a general rule when working with EF entities and MVC/WebAPI, do not mix entities with view models. You cannot simply use a hidden input an entity, what that column will contain is effectively the object.ToString(), so essentially &quot;Your.Model.Namespace.Genome&quot;.</p>
<p>To get something like that working, you would need hidden inputs for each property of the GenomeMatchingViewModel.MatchBase for MVC to re-construct the GenomeBase. This is why your MatchTarget works, there are inputs for each bound value to re-construct it, however any and every property that <em>doesn't</em> have an input will be #null/default, so it can still be a trap as application behaviour evolves. You can assume you have a complete object, but later find it is actually incomplete in certain cases after an instance is saturated by MVC.</p>
<p>Alternatively for complex objects (again, not recommended for entities as I'll outline below) your view model can instead expose a JSON serialization to bind your hidden input to:</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>public string GenomeBaseJson { get; set; }

private GenomeBase? _genomeBase
[JsonIgnore] // In case the VM is ever serialized
public GenomeBase
{ 
    get =&gt; _genomeBase ??= JsonConvert.DeserializeObject&lt;GenomeBase&gt;(GenomBaseJson);
    set 
    {
        _genomeBase = value;
        GenomeBaseJson = JsonConvert.SerializeObject(_genomeBase);
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>Where your hidden input binds to GenomeBaseJson. This way the hidden input is storing the JSON representation as string, and when you access the GenomeBase object, it will deserialize the JSON, caching the instance for repeated access, similarly updating the Json value when setting.</p>
<p>However, it is advisable to keep view models pure and POCOs rather than embedding entities within them. Entities ideally should not live outside of the scope of the <code>DbContext</code> instance that is tracking it. Working with untracked entities gets messy and can very easily lead to issues you don't expect, including behaviour that manifests at runtime such as tracking related errors (an instance with this Id is already tracked) or unintended data overwrites.</p>
<p>In your example, there is little point in passing the entire Genome to the view with the view model. Instead you could simply pass the GenomeId and possibly any other property of the Genome that your view might display, like a Name, etc.  The VM should be a POCO that is safe to serialize, so no <code>DbContext</code> in it, or the MatchTarget, that logic belongs in a controller or separate service layer. When you go to create a new record and associate it to a Genome, use the DbContext to locate the Genome by ID, same as when you set up the match view model, and associate or create, if that is a valid scenario. Generally though I would also recommend avoiding combining scenarios like &quot;Upsert&quot; operations. If you expect to use an existing Genome that should be one workflow through the controller so that it can validate that an expected GenomeId exists, where there is a separate workflow and view model for scenarios where a new Genome is created that can enforce those validations. (I.e. value uniqueness, etc.) Combining them can lead to confusing scenarios where the code can expect new values and end up triggering scenarios where existing rows get updated in ways you don't intend.  I.e. passing every field from Genome for the Insert scenario of an Upsert, but those values end up overwriting/renaming values in Update scenarios which you don't intend to happen, but occur due to a bug or tampering from users. Code paths/functions should do one thing, and one thing well. &quot;Upsert&quot; is two things, Update or Insert.</p>

Q: write ZIP with PHP from MSSQL IMAGE field retrieved as BINARY STREAM A: <p>This should solve the problem:</p>

<pre><code>$file = fopen('test.zip', 'w');
while (!feof($img)) {
    $chunk = fread($img, 1024);
    fwrite($file, $chunk);
}
fclose($img);
fclose($file);
</code></pre>
